            content: '<p>Italy and the Balkans felt the impact of a record-breaking heatwave in late June 2026, causing hundreds of heat-related deaths across Europe and raising fears of spreading wildfires. According to Reuters reporting from Rome and Belgrade, temperatures across parts of Italy and the Balkans reached the highest levels in modern weather record history.</p><p>Al Jazeera reported that older people remain the most at risk during the heatwave, with excess deaths rising sharply across Europe. Health authorities in several Italian cities issued emergency heat warnings, urging elderly and vulnerable residents to stay indoors during the hottest hours of the day.</p><p>The heatwave continues a historic atmospheric "heat dome" phenomenon that has gripped Europe since late May 2026, breaking June temperature records in countries including the UK, France, and Germany before shifting eastward toward Italy and the Balkans.</p><p>World Weather Attribution, an independent climate research group, said fossil fuel emissions have significantly worsened European heatwaves over recent decades. Their studies show such heatwave patterns are now far more frequent and intense compared to decades past due to climate change.</p><p>Fire authorities in Italy and Balkan countries have raised alert levels amid heightened wildfire risk from extremely dry vegetation conditions, with some regions already implementing temporary bans on activities that could spark fires in forested and dry land areas.</p>',
